{
 "duration": 54.951,
 "sentences": [
  {
   "index": 0,
   "start": 0.25,
   "end": 2.841,
   "text": "One git clone could run a hacker's code on your machine."
  },
  {
   "index": 1,
   "start": 3.191,
   "end": 7.608,
   "text": "This week, Git patched six vulnerabilities. The worst is rated high severity."
  },
  {
   "index": 2,
   "start": 7.988,
   "end": 14.152,
   "text": "Here's the trick. Before Git writes a file, it checks the path, so nothing can overwrite its own .git data."
  },
  {
   "index": 3,
   "start": 14.512,
   "end": 24.114,
   "text": "But the attacker uses an extremely long path. Its length gets truncated. So it passes the check under its full name, then gets written under a shorter one: .git"
  },
  {
   "index": 4,
   "start": 24.474,
   "end": 29.856,
   "text": "Inside a submodule, that file becomes a symlink, pointing at a folder of malicious hooks."
  },
  {
   "index": 5,
   "start": 30.216,
   "end": 35.346,
   "text": "When Git uses that folder, the attacker's code runs. On your laptop, or your CI runner."
  },
  {
   "index": 6,
   "start": 35.726,
   "end": 40.913,
   "text": "It only triggers with --recurse-submodules, which pipelines and coding agents use all the time."
  },
  {
   "index": 7,
   "start": 41.293,
   "end": 45.589,
   "text": "The fix: update to Git 2.56.1, or a patched backport."
  },
  {
   "index": 8,
   "start": 45.949,
   "end": 50.779,
   "text": "Can't update yet? Clone without submodules first, and inspect .gitmodules"
  },
  {
   "index": 9,
   "start": 51.199,
   "end": 54.051,
   "text": "A repo is code from a stranger. Treat it that way."
  }
 ],
